NNAAMMEE
     nnoollooggiinn — politely refuse a login

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     nnoollooggiinn

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The nnoollooggiinn is intended as a replacement shell field for accounts that
     have been disabled.

     When executed, nnoollooggiinn first writes about the login attempt to syslog(3)
     and then displays a message that an account is not available.

     To disable all logins, investigate nologin(5).

EEXXIITT SSTTAATTUUSS
     The nnoollooggiinn utility always exits non-zero.

EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
     Here is a demonstration of executing nnoollooggiinn:

         $ nologin
         This account is currently not available.
         $ tail -n 1 /var/log/messages
         Mar 30 21:53:07 example.org nologin[65992]: Attempted login by beastie on /dev/pts/18

SSEEEE AALLSSOO
     login(1), nologin(5)

HHIISSTTOORRYY
     The nnoollooggiinn utility appeared in 4.4BSD.

